[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Transaction Isolation Levels and Anomalies in PostgreSQL"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Concurrency control is critical for maintaining database integrity when multiple transactions execute simultaneously. PostgreSQL implements the SQL standard's transaction isolation levels using Multi-Version Concurrency Control (MVCC), which prevents readers from blocking writers and writers from blocking readers."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Concurrency Anomalies"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before diving into isolation levels, it is essential to understand the anomalies that can occur when concurrent transactions interact improperly:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dirty Read: A transaction reads data written by a concurrent uncommitted transaction."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Non-Repeatable Read: A transaction re-reads data it has previously read and finds that data has been modified by another committed transaction."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Phantom Read: A transaction re-runs a query returning a set of rows satisfying a search condition and finds that the set has changed due to newly inserted or deleted rows by another committed transaction."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Serialization Anomaly: The result of a group of concurrent committed transactions is inconsistent with all possible serial executions of those transactions."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"PostgreSQL Isolation Levels"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"PostgreSQL supports three of the four standard SQL isolation levels. Note that in PostgreSQL, Read Uncommitted behaves like Read Committed."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Read Committed: The default isolation level in PostgreSQL. A query only sees data committed before the query began."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Repeatable Read: All queries in the current transaction see a snapshot established as of the start of the first query (not the transaction start)."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Serializable: The strictest level. It emulates full serial execution of transactions, guaranteeing that any concurrent execution is completely safe from serialization anomalies."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Demonstrating Read Committed vs Repeatable Read"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"To set the isolation level for a transaction, use the following SQL syntax:"},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"BEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a scenario where Transaction A updates an account balance, but has not yet committed. Under Read Committed isolation, Transaction B cannot see these uncommitted changes, thus preventing Dirty Reads. However, if Transaction A commits its changes, a subsequent SELECT statement inside Transaction B will see the updated values, demonstrating a Non-Repeatable Read."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"-- Session 1
BEGIN;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 1;

-- Session 2 (Read Committed)
BEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL READ COMMITTED;
SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1; -- Returns old value

-- Session 1 commits
COMMIT;

-- Session 2 executes same query again
SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1; -- Returns new value (Non-Repeatable Read)"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling Serialization Failures"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"When using the Serializable isolation level, PostgreSQL uses Serializable Snapshot Isolation (SSI). SSI detects dependencies between transactions and will automatically abort a transaction if a serialization failure occurs, returning an error."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"ERROR: could not serialize access due to read/write dependencies among transactions"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Applications utilizing Serializable isolation must include retry logic to handle these aborted transactions gracefully and re-run them from the beginning."}]
